A||||11||SteveJ||oil filter comparison||||11||
Z||000000||SteveJ||08-06-2001||08:57 AM||||I was over on the Neon message board which I found using Boardreader.com ( sorry Cliff I have been consorting with the enemy ) and found a link to a site where they tested oil filters by taking them apart.<BR> <A HREF="http://members.nbci.com/minimopar/oilfilterstudy.html" TARGET=_blank>http://members.nbci.com/minimopar/oilfilterstudy.html</A> <P>Now I never did buy Fram.||||reg||11
Z||000001||PBJ||08-06-2001||10:54 AM||||Interesting info.  <P>Did you's also know that during the "lack of maint" recall for the 4 cyl GM changed the recommended filter from the pf47 to the larger pf52 (ac delco #'s) for increased oil capacity.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000002||Leadfoot||08-06-2001||01:59 PM||||My friend showed me a site a couple of years ago that said don't by Fram because they can break down due to cheap material quality (something like that).  Since then I have never bought Fram, and now I am glad I haven't.||||reg||1
Z||000003||JohnF||08-06-2001||03:35 PM||||Odd, but Michigan Fiero site for recalls says correct filter for ALL Fieros is to be PF51 (not 52)? as changed by recall 88-C-23.||||reg||9
Z||000004||Bruce||08-06-2001||04:25 PM||||I couldn't find any of the recommended brands at my local Kragen's, so I decided to stimulate the economy by buying a K&N oil filter, after I returned the Fram 3980. Has the stock market moved up as a result?||||reg||1
Z||000005||theogre||08-06-2001||09:12 PM||||PF-51 is an extinct number. PF-52 replaces it. (xxx3980 in the Fram number system)<P>All the filters on the market meet or exceed current new car standards for filtration and reliability.<P>testing by qualified labs has generally shown little diferance in filter performance between brands over the years. Nearly any filter you buy today will be better than what GM originally specified for the car.<P>I've read the linked site above. What looks like "cardboard" is more likely some type of resin/epoxy impregnated material. It's a heck of allot tougher than it looks. <P>He clearly says there is no test involved.<P>It's interesting reading but I don't think it has any real relivance in how well the filters do their job and last in street application. In a race application I wouldn't use anything but WIX or one of the other heavy duty filters. <p>[This message has been edited by theogre (edited 08-06-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000006||SteveJ||08-07-2001||10:24 AM||||Well I agree with everything you say but have to point out that on one of my oil changes some time ago I found the filter element clunking around inside the case. Obviously it had been nonfunctional. I had been using your logic of any filter must be good enough. It might have been a Kmart house brand, I just can't remember.<BR>Fram and its rebranded clones are evidently of lower quality just on inspection alone. Pennzoil uses these filters for pete's sake. I had one put in my son's car last year at one of their quick oil change places.<P>It's a classic case of buyer beware except that some unethical business forces know we won't take them apart and check their quality. We have to buy based on the brand and fancy box advertising. Given the choice I'll take the one that is made better even if the lesser meets the established standards.||||reg||1
Z||000007||theogre||08-07-2001||07:15 PM||||I never use quick lube places. They always buy the cheapest stuff they can find. I know I worked for one. I've delt with the mess from others. The can on them is often so thin that they actually streatch.<P>I've been working in cars for 25+ years... I've handled hundreds of filters at least from all brands. I've use plenty of Fram AC MotorCraft and others. I've never seen a filter fail unless:<P>A. it was a really cheap noname/rebrand POS. Usually from a quick lube place. Allot of these things come from god knows what little sweatshop.<P>B. The oil system was way over pressure. Usually happens in race applications but can happen to street vehicles. This is why filters like WIX are built so heavy it's not funny.<P>C. The filter was flooded with trash. (happens often on farm and construction equipment that isn't cared for.)<P>D. The oil was really cold. Like 20-30 bewlow zero F. This happens if the bypass valve fails.||||reg||1
Z||000008||SteveJ||08-07-2001||08:56 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by theogre:<BR><B>I never use quick lube places. </B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>I've been changing my own oil all my life but with a son in graduate school in Illinois who cares little about car maintenance I was forced to use a quick change place during one of my visits. If I didn't change the oil in his car he never would. Maybe he will support me in my old age. . .||||reg||1
Z||000009||JohnF||08-08-2001||10:14 AM||||Hey, GM Goodwrench - 29 minutes or it's free!<P>Any certified GM dealer @ $21.95 (and here about every other one is FREE). Great price.<BR>Top quality, good prices.  Why do anything else?||||reg||12
Z||000010||StuGood||08-08-2001||11:33 AM||||First oil change on my newest Fiero, I used a NAPA brand filter, and I'm telling you, it had the thinnest housing of any filter I ever saw!  Almost like it wasn't there - I could actually see the filter element!<P>Oh, by the way, it's a 1988 Duke, so the filter <B>has</B> no housing.  Nevermind... <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">  <P> ||||reg||12
Z||000011||thomas_l||08-08-2001||03:04 PM||||I managed a quick lube for a few years. We used cheap, re-branded Champ filters. On 1000s of changes, the ONLY failures I saw were due to:<P>1. Wrong filter (RTFM!)<BR>2. Double gasket. (peel the old one off first!)<BR>3. Engine oil system problem.(Yes they will blow up like a steel ballon when the oil pump bypass is stuck)<P>I would agree with Ogre - Wix makes the best quality filters if that's something you worry about. I just go to the Three Brothers and get a plain AC PF-52. If my GT swallows the cheap filter that will be my excuse to put in a Northstar   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>BTW - Wix makes NAPA filters. The cheap Silverline filters are no better than anything else. The Gold filters are the same thing you get in a regular Wix box.<p>[This message has been edited by thomas_l (edited 08-08-2001).]||||reg||1
